  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 927 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nQUIET START TO HOLIDAY WEEKEND:\n\nIf you enjoyed Friday's weather, you will also be a fan of \nSaturday's as the weather pattern remains largely unchanged. \nModest moisture has pooled along the Divide and may spawn \nisolated afternoon showers and storms, but the majority of the \nforecast area will remain. Temperatures remain seasonable. \n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES INCREASE LATE SUNDAY INTO MONDAY:\n\nWhile previous model runs suggested a surge of monsoonal \nmoisture into the Four Corners region come Sunday, recent trends\nhave slowed the arrival time of rich moisture to late Sunday \nevening into Monday. Hi-res models are in good agreement for \nthis later arrival time as well. With moisture moving in past \npeak heating, this may delay the risk for flash flooding from \nheavy rainfall to Monday. Even so, if much of the area is \nalready blanketed with clouds come Monday morning, it may be \nhard to build instability which would also reduce the risk for \nflash flooding. While the threat for flash flooding seems to be \non the decline on a broader scale, soils may remain saturated in\nsome areas after multiple rounds of heavy rainfall last \nweekend. It may not take much additional precipitation to \ntrigger additional debris flows. Recent burn scars and areas \nwith steep terrain will remain susceptible as well. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nModel spread continues to lead to a lower confidence forecast \nbeyond Monday. The GEFS retains richer moisture along and south \nof I-70, while the ECMWF ENS has the moisture receding to the \nsoutheast on Tuesday. However, later in the week much of the \nWest will likely see an uptick in moisture as the remnants of \nHurricane Marie advect inland. Otherwise, high pressure looks to\ndrive the forecast through much of the week ahead.",
